{
 "id": "2024-chen-ceramic",
 "name": "Ceramic Tile Visual Comfort: 12-item rating of 6 rooms",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the original renderings: six renderings of the same empty room with a window and a small table and chairs, made in Kujiale and identical except for the floor tiles, in the paper's 3 x 2 design of tile brightness (light, medium and dark toned) and texture (textured marble pattern and non-textured). Participants see the six rooms one at a time and answer, for each, the paper's 12 visual comfort questions (Table 4), each with its five labelled answers from the negative to the positive end (e.g. Do the ceramic tiles here feel ugly or beautiful? Very ugly ... Very beautiful): physiological comfort (temperature, brightness, spaciousness, vitality), aesthetic comfort (order, beauty, refinement, neatness) and emotional comfort (pleasure, intimacy, calm, relaxation). Gender and age are asked first. Rebuilt from Chen, Cheng & Guo (2024), Evaluation Method and the Influence of Visual Comfort of Ceramic Tiles in Indoor Environment—A Study Based on the Delphi and AHP, Buildings 14(9): 2829, with the renderings cropped from its Figure 1 and the questions from its Table 4 (CC BY 4.0). Image ids: a_light_textured, b_medium_textured, c_dark_textured, d_light_nontextured, e_medium_nontextured, f_dark_nontextured (samples a-f of Figure 1); answers are in comfort, keyed by image id, with items C1-C12 scored 1-5; the paper's AHP weights are stored in the page's original-study block. Analysis as in the paper: weighted visual comfort score per participant and room (sum of each answer times the paper's AHP weight, e.g. brightness 0.1632, relaxation 0.1286, spaciousness 0.1276) and per dimension, averaged per room, and repeated-measures ANOVA with brightness and texture within subjects and gender between subjects; the paper (342 valid paper questionnaires from college students, mean age 20.2) found the light-toned non-textured tiles most comfortable (3.949) and the dark-toned textured tiles least (2.657), brightness and texture significant for the total score, and men rating aesthetic comfort lower than women. Differences from the original study: (1) the paper used paper questionnaires handed to college students; here the survey is online and anyone can take part; (2) the paper does not state the order of the rooms, so here they are shown in individually randomized order; (3) the gender and age questions and the instruction text are ours; the paper reports gender and mean age only; (4) the renderings are the figure copies (about 1220 px wide).",
